Pure dyadic n=16 radial table
  For owner u=2^k, every endpoint has 2-adic depth 4+k. A protector p=2^j*q translates the ray upward by j edges.
  owner=2   depth={2:5} lower_union=  2/4   j=0[2:1]
  owner=4   depth={2:6} lower_union=  4/8   j=0[2:2] j=1[4:1]
  owner=8   depth={2:7} lower_union=  8/16  j=0[2:4] j=1[4:2] j=2[8:1]
  owner=16  depth={2:8} lower_union= 32/32  j=0[4:8] j=1[4:4] j=2[8:2] j=3[16:1]
  owner=32  depth={2:9} lower_union= 64/64  j=0[8:16] j=1[8:8] j=2[8:4] j=3[16:2] j=4[32:1]
  owner=64  depth={2:10} lower_union=128/128 j=0[16:32] j=1[16:16] j=2[16:8] j=3[16:4] j=4[32:2] j=5[64:1]
  Read this as a BT-tree warning: local radial descent is real, but u>=16 has a stable nine-fan that can close one branch locally.

Synthesis
  Bruhat-Tits descent is a sharper language for the existing Bohr-boundary descent.  Unit endpoints are first-layer boundary rays at the primes dividing n.  THM-360 says only an n-gate moves all of those rays back to the root.
  But an n-gate creates its own endpoints at depth 2*v_p(n)+v_p(q). So gate repair is not deletion; it is debt export into child vertices of the local p-adic tree.
  The n=16 dyadic theorem is exactly the rank-one BT lab: diagonal translation by p=2^j controls endpoint counts, while odd residue classes decide which branches are hit.  The next proof target is a global product-tree potential whose boundary strictly increases under every attempted all-protected core.
